requirements like R9.2 and R10.x, stty is on R7.2 and R7.3, while pr and ptx are tackling earlier requirements, suggesting they're earlier in their implementation cycles.\n\nThere's a discrepancy though: pr and ptx show \"not started\" in code_status but have R1.x requirements already completed, which shouldn't be possible. The requirement_states only lists \"ready\" requirements, so anything missing is either done, in progress, or skipped. This suggests the code_status snapshot might be outdated compared to the current requirement_states, and I should trust the source code over the prose descriptions.\n\nSince I can't directly check the source code, I'll go with what the requirement_states show as the most current information. Now I'm formulating the final task list with enough detail for a stitch agent working independently, starting with the weight calculations to verify each task fits within the 4-point limit. Identifying the remaining requirements for future batches across the utilities, then organizing the YAML output alphabetically by command path with all tasks marked as independent since they're separate utilities.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the ordering strategy—ts is nearly complete with only four requirements left, while pr and ptx have significantly more work ahead, so finishing ts first makes practical sense even though all are in the same release. For the task descriptions, I need to be thorough about what the stitch agent needs: which SRD sections to reference, what existing code to examine, the specific requirements to implement, which files to create or modify, any design decisions, and clear acceptance criteria.\n\nOne concern though—pr and ptx show \"not started\" for code status, but if R1.x requirements are already completed, there should be existing code. I need to clarify whether these are truly new packages requiring a contract task first, or if there's existing implementation I'm not seeing.\n\nLooking at the recent commit history, I can see the orchestrator has been processing requirements and marking them in progress, which suggests the packages do exist and have been worked on. The code status might just be outdated from the analysis snapshot. I'm thinking through what the requirements likely cover for each package. Without diving into the SRD files themselves, I'll keep the descriptions general enough that the stitch agent can read the specs and fill in the details, but specific enough to guide the work. For the required reading, I need to list the SRD files, existing implementation files, and any test utilities—so for ts, stty, pr, and ptx, that means pointing to their respective spec files, main implementations, and test files.\n\nWriting the YAML output now with task descriptions that reference specific SRD requirement items so the stitch agent knows exactly what to implement. I'm laying out the implementation plan with specific requirements for each feature, design decisions to maintain consistency with the existing codebase, and acceptance criteria to verify the work is complete. I'm realizing that without actually reading the SRD, I can't be more specific than pointing to the requirement numbers. Looking at the git log pattern, similar tasks use concise titles that reference the SRD sections, letting the stitch agent fill in the details. I'll keep my descriptions minimal and focused on the SRD references rather than trying to invent specifics.\n\nNow I'm checking whether earlier requirement batches like R1.x were already completed for pr and ptx—the fact that only \"ready\" items show up in the requirement states and R2.1 is the first one listed suggests the R1.x work is done. So my final task breakdown is: finish ts with R9.2 and R10.1-R10.3, complete stty with R7.2-R7.3, continue pr starting at R2.1-R2.3, and begin ptx with R2.1-R2.3.\n\nI'm being cautious about including pr R3.1 since I can't confirm its weight—if it's weight 4, adding it would exceed my capacity, so I'm leaving it out. 